Here's how to implement this in .NET MAUI Shell:

## 1. Override `OnBackButtonPressed` + custom toolbar button

```csharp
// EditProductPage.xaml.cs
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    public EditProductPage()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        Shell.SetBackButtonBehavior(this, new BackButtonBehavior
        {
            TextOverride = "Cancel",
            Command = new Command(async () => await TryGoBack())
        });
    }

    // Handles Android/hardware back button
    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (!_hasUnsavedChanges)
            return base.OnBackButtonPressed();

        // Fire-and-forget the dialog; return true to cancel navigation
        MainThread.BeginInvokeOnMainThread(async () => await TryGoBack());
        return true; // prevents default back navigation
    }

    private async Task TryGoBack()
    {
        if (_hasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
                "Unsaved Changes",
                "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
                "Discard", "Stay");

            if (!leave) return;
        }

        await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
    }
}
```

## 2. Track unsaved changes

Set `_hasUnsavedChanges = true` whenever a field changes:

```xml
<!-- EditProductPage.xaml -->
<Entry Text="{Binding ProductName}"
       TextChanged="OnFieldChanged" />
```

```csharp
private void OnFieldChanged(object sender, TextChangedEventArgs e)
{
    _hasUnsavedChanges = true;
}
```

## Key points

| Mechanism | What it covers |
|---|---|
| `OnBackButtonPressed()` | Android hardware/gesture back |
| `BackButtonBehavior.Command` | Shell navbar back button (iOS & Android) |

- `OnBackButtonPressed` must return `true` synchronously to cancel navigation — that's why the async dialog is dispatched separately.
- `BackButtonBehavior` replaces the default arrow with a "Cancel" label and routes through the same `TryGoBack()` logic.
- On iOS, swipe-back gesture isn't intercepted by either mechanism. To block it, set `Shell.PresentationMode="ModalAnimated"` on the page route, or use a custom renderer to disable the gesture.
